---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base
  title="Writing async-like code in durable execution"
  description="Durable execution gives you retries and replay without callbacks. Getting there in Mojo meant closing a hole in the run block, bounding its retries, and deleting an API that deadlocked."
>
  <article class="section post">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">Durable execution</p>
      <h1 class="post__title">Writing async-like code in durable execution</h1>
      <p class="post__meta">
        <time datetime="2026-09-02">2 September 2026</time> ·
        <a href="https://github.com/magmalake/restate.mojo" rel="noopener">restate.mojo</a>
      </p>

      <p class="lede">
        A durable execution engine promises something that sounds impossible:
        write ordinary straight-line code, and if the process dies halfway
        through, it resumes without redoing the parts that already succeeded.
        No callbacks, no state machine, no reconciliation job. This is what
        that looks like in Mojo, and what it took to make the failure paths
        honest.
      </p>

      <h2>The shape of the promise</h2>

      <p>
        Here is an order flow. It reserves stock, charges a card, and ships.
        Read it as if nothing can go wrong:
      </p>

      <pre class="post__code"><code>{`var reserved = reserve(app, inv)
var charged  = charge(app, inv)
var shipped  = ship(app, inv)
app.complete(inv, String(reserved, " / ", charged, " / ", shipped))`}</code></pre>

      <p>
        Now let the card decline. The invocation fails, Restate re-delivers it,
        and the handler runs again from the top — but <em>reserve does not run
        a second time</em>. Its result was journaled the first time through and
        is replayed from the log:
      </p>

      <pre class="post__code"><code>{`--- attempt 1 at t+0s
[attempt 1] execute  reserve  -> res-order-1-c4ea8c
[attempt 1] execute  charge   -> boom: card declined (retrying in-place)
--- attempt 2 at t+0s
[attempt 2] REPLAY   reserve  -> res-order-1-c4ea8c
[attempt 2] execute  charge   -> boom: card declined (retrying in-place)
--- attempt 3 at t+2s
[attempt 3] REPLAY   reserve  -> res-order-1-c4ea8c
[attempt 3] execute  charge   -> chg-order-1-b40aa
[attempt 3] execute  ship     -> shipped
[attempt 3] done`}</code></pre>

      <p>
        The reservation id ends in a nanosecond timestamp, so a second
        execution could not have produced the same string. That it is identical
        across three attempts is the journal doing its job, not luck. This is
        the whole pitch: retry semantics you would otherwise hand-roll with a
        state table and an idempotency key, expressed as a function that reads
        top to bottom.
      </p>

      <h2>Where the journal boundary goes</h2>

      <p>
        The mechanism is a <code>run</code> block. You ask the journal whether
        this step already happened; if not, you do the work and record the
        result:
      </p>

      <pre class="post__code"><code>{`var replayed = app.run_enter(inv)
if replayed:
    return replayed.value()          # already done, here is what it returned
var value = charge_the_card()
return app.run_exit(inv, value)      # journal it`}</code></pre>

      <p>
        Which raises the question this post is really about: what happens when
        <code>charge_the_card()</code> throws?
      </p>

      <p>
        In our first version, nothing good. There was no way to close a run
        block that had failed. Raising between <code>run_enter</code> and
        <code>run_exit</code> left the block open, and the journal kept
        expecting an exit that never came. Every later attempt died the same
        way:
      </p>

      <pre class="post__code"><code>{`restate.mojo protocol error: expected rst_run_exit`}</code></pre>

      <p>
        The invocation could then neither complete nor terminally fail. It
        retried that protocol error forever. The workaround was to do the
        fallible call <em>before</em> opening the block and journal the result
        only once you had one — which works, and leaves a window where a crash
        after a successful charge but before the journal write charges the card
        twice on retry.
      </p>

      <p>
        That window is exactly what a run block exists to close, so the
        workaround was the wrong shape. The fix was to expose what the
        underlying SDK already had: a way to fail the block.
      </p>

      <h2>Three ways to fail, and they are not interchangeable</h2>

      <p>
        The interesting part is that "it failed" is not one thing. There are
        three, and choosing wrong is the most common way to misuse a durable
        execution engine:
      </p>

      <table class="post__table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Call</th><th>Journaled?</th><th>What happens</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><code>run_fail(terminal=False)</code></td>
            <td>no</td>
            <td>the step runs again — a blip, a timeout, a 503</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>run_fail(terminal=True)</code></td>
            <td>yes, as a failure</td>
            <td>raised so you can compensate and finish</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>app.fail(inv, msg)</code></td>
            <td>ends the invocation</td>
            <td>the caller gets an error, nothing is retried</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <p>
        Abandoning a permanently broken invocation retries it forever. Failing
        a transient one throws away work that would have succeeded on the next
        attempt. A malformed order will not become well-formed by being tried
        again; a card network having a bad minute will.
      </p>

      <p>
        There is a fourth state that is not a failure at all, and it is the one
        that bit us while writing the example. Between retries an invocation
        can be <em>suspended</em> — parked on a durable timer, to be resumed
        later. That arrives as an exception like any other. Catch it as if the
        step had failed and you will compensate for work that was merely
        waiting:
      </p>

      <pre class="post__code"><code>{`except e:
    if is_suspended(e):
        raise e          # not a failure — let the driver park it
    compensate()`}</code></pre>

      <h2>Retries need a budget</h2>

      <p>
        Once a step can fail transiently, something has to decide how long to
        keep trying. Our first version left that to Restate's server-side
        policy, which retries indefinitely. That is right for a payment and
        wrong for a courtesy email nobody is waiting on, and there was no way
        to say which one you had.
      </p>

      <pre class="post__code"><code>{`var slot = app.run_enter_policy(inv, initial_delay_ms=250, max_attempts=3)`}</code></pre>

      <p>
        Three attempts a quarter-second apart, then the block fails terminally
        and the handler carries on without the notification. The order still
        gets placed:
      </p>

      <pre class="post__code"><code>{`--- notify attempt 5
[attempt 5] execute  notify   -> smtp timeout
--- notify attempt 6
[attempt 6] execute  notify   -> smtp timeout
--- notify attempt 7
[attempt 7] execute  notify   -> smtp timeout
  gave up after 3 tries: Terminal error [500]: smtp timeout`}</code></pre>

      <h2>The part Mojo made harder, and the part it made easier</h2>

      <p>
        Handlers run on a pool of OS threads, and a thread start routine takes
        a C function pointer and one <code>void *</code>. So a handler has to
        be <em>thin</em> — non-capturing. Anything it needs beyond the
        invocation travels through that pointer, which for a while meant this:
      </p>

      <pre class="post__code"><code>{`var live = AtomicCounter.at(Int(ctx) + C_LIVE * 8)`}</code></pre>

      <p>
        A byte offset into an untyped block, with a <code>C_LIVE</code>
        constant that had to stay in sync with a layout written down nowhere.
        Rust developers will recognise the era: before <code>async fn</code>,
        you hand-wrote the state machine that the compiler now generates for
        you.
      </p>

      <p>
        The fix is a typed view of that pointer. <code>Ctx[T]</code> carries
        the state's type across the thin boundary, so a handler says what it
        means and the compiler works out the offsets:
      </p>

      <pre class="post__code"><code>{`def handle_hold(app: App, inv: Invocation, worker: Int, ctx: Ctx[Stats]) raises:
    var live = counter(ctx[].live)
    live.increment()`}</code></pre>

      <p>
        It types the sharing; it does not synchronise it. Every worker gets the
        same <code>T</code> concurrently, so shared fields are still atomics.
        But the byte arithmetic is gone, and — the point — this is the same
        program the eventual API keeps. When Mojo can carry captures across a
        thread boundary, the parameter disappears and <code>ctx[].live</code>
        becomes <code>live</code>. A rename, not a rewrite.
      </p>

      <h2>Compile-time code earns its keep</h2>

      <p>
        Mojo's <code>comptime</code> turned out to remove more boilerplate than
        expected. A service used to declare itself twice — a list of handler
        names passed to the app, and an <code>if inv.handler == …</code> chain
        — with nothing keeping the two in step. Adding a handler meant
        remembering both, and forgetting one failed at runtime on the first
        request that reached it. I made that exact mistake while writing the
        example for this post.
      </p>

      <pre class="post__code"><code>{`def handle_process(app: App, inv: Invocation, worker: Int, ctx: Ctx[Flow]) raises:
    ...

def main() raises:
    var flow = Flow(...)
    _ = App.run[Flow, __functions_in_module()]("Orders", flow)`}</code></pre>

      <p>
        Every <code>handle_*</code> in the module is discovered at compile
        time; both the registration list and the dispatch are derived from the
        same functions, so they cannot disagree. There is no list to keep in
        step because there is no list.
      </p>

      <p>
        This is also the honest answer to "should we generate the transitional
        code with a macro?" — we already do. The adapter that bridges a typed
        handler to the thin one the thread pool needs is a parameterised
        function the compiler instantiates per handler. Nobody writes it.
        <code>comptime</code> shrinks the hand-written layer to nearly nothing;
        it cannot make a runtime closure cross a thread boundary, which is the
        one thing still waiting on the language.
      </p>

      <h2>Deleting the API that looked simplest</h2>

      <p>
        The first version of this binding had a single-threaded driver: you
        wrote the loop yourself, pulled invocations, and handled them. It was
        the simplest thing that could work and it made a lovely first example.
      </p>

      <p>
        It also deadlocked the moment a handler called another handler served
        by the same process. There was no second thread to run the callee, and
        nothing in the API said so — it passed every test that did not happen
        to make that call, and hung the first time someone added one.
      </p>

      <p>
        It survived as long as it did because writing handlers the other way
        meant reaching state through a raw pointer. Once <code>Ctx[T]</code>
        removed that cost, the only thing the loop still offered was a trap. It
        is gone in 0.3.0. Serving on a pool is the only shape, and the default
        is one worker per core.
      </p>

      <p>
        There is a version of API design where you keep the simple thing
        alongside the correct thing and document the difference. We tried that
        for a release. The documentation was accurate and it did not help,
        because the failure mode is silent and arrives long after the choice.
      </p>

      <h2>What it adds up to</h2>

      <p>
        The code at the top of this post is real, and it survives a process
        dying between any two lines. The work was not in making the happy path
        elegant — durable execution hands you that. It was in making every
        failure path say what it means: a step that should be retried, a step
        that is finished failing, an invocation that is a lost cause, and a
        pause that is not a failure at all.
      </p>

      <p>
        <a href="https://github.com/magmalake/restate.mojo" rel="noopener">restate.mojo</a>
        is Apache-2.0, built on the official Restate Rust SDK behind a C ABI,
        and the example above is <code>examples/failures.mojo</code> — it runs
        against a real Restate server in about ten seconds.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>

